@{
    RootModule        = 'ZellijTerminal.psm1'
    ModuleVersion     = '0.7.10'
    GUID              = 'cbeb77a8-d47e-4f13-aa58-231e6cadee15'
    Author            = 'zt contributors'
    CompanyName       = ''
    Copyright         = '(c) 2026 zt contributors. MIT licence.'
    Description       = 'zt - register, start, stop and restart the workspaces you run inside one Zellij session on Windows. Definitions live in git; per-device paths and live state do not. Claude Code sessions register themselves via the hook; arbitrary PowerShell commands work the same way.'

    # 7.0, and the distinction matters.
    #
    # The SCRIPTS in scripts\ are deliberately 5.1-clean - no ternary, no ??, no
    # && - because the Claude Code hook invokes them under powershell.exe and
    # must keep doing so. Their headers say as much and it is enforced by test.
    #
    # This MODULE is a different contract. install.ps1 junctions it onto the
    # pwsh 7 module path, which Windows PowerShell does not scan, so on 5.1 `zt`
    # never autoloads no matter how compatible the body happens to be. Declaring
    # 5.1 here described the syntax and lied about the product: it let a 5.1 user
    # satisfy the manifest and still have nothing work, with no error naming the
    # cause. The floor is what the thing needs to actually run.
    PowerShellVersion = '7.0'

    # Core only. There is no Desktop story - see above.
    CompatiblePSEditions = @('Core')

    # Listed by name, no wildcards: that is what lets PowerShell autoload the
    # module the first time `zt` is typed.
    FunctionsToExport = @(
        'Invoke-ZellijTerminal',
        'Get-ZellijTerminal',
        'Select-ZellijTerminal',
        'Register-ZellijTerminal',
        'Unregister-ZellijTerminal',
        'Publish-ZellijTerminal',
        'Get-ZellijTerminalRoot',
        'Set-ZellijTerminalRoot',
        'Start-ZellijTerminal',
        'Stop-ZellijTerminal',
        'Restart-ZellijTerminal',
        'Remove-ZellijTerminalTab',
        'Sync-ZellijTerminal',
        'Set-ZellijTerminalWaiting',
        'Switch-ZellijTerminal',
        'Connect-ZellijTerminal',
        'Suspend-ZellijTerminal',
        'Resume-ZellijTerminal',
        'Edit-ZellijTerminalConfig',
        'Test-ZellijTerminalConfig',
        'Test-ZellijTerminalPad',
        'Debug-ZellijTerminalPad',
        'Install-ZellijTerminalPad',
        'Set-ZellijTerminalPadDevice',
        'Uninstall-ZellijTerminalPad',
        'Test-ZellijTerminal',
        'Get-ZellijTerminalDiagnostic',
        'Test-ZellijTerminalPaste',
        'Repair-ZellijTerminalPaste',
        'Get-ZellijTerminalHotkey',
        'Get-ZellijTerminalSession',
        'Remove-ZellijTerminalSession',
        'Add-ZellijTerminalDock',
        'Get-ZellijTerminalDock',
        'Install-ZellijTerminal',
        'Uninstall-ZellijTerminal',
        'Export-ZellijTerminal',
        'Import-ZellijTerminal',
        'Start-ZellijTerminalSetup',
        'Show-ZellijTerminalPadGuide',
        'Show-ZellijTerminalPaletteGuide'
    )
    CmdletsToExport   = @()
    VariablesToExport = @()
    AliasesToExport   = @('zt', 'zac')

    PrivateData       = @{
        PSData = @{
            Tags         = @('Zellij', 'ClaudeCode', 'MacroPad', 'Windows', 'Workspace', 'Productivity')
            LicenseUri   = 'https://github.com/ztpwsh/zellij-terminal/blob/main/LICENSE'
            ProjectUri   = 'https://github.com/ztpwsh/zellij-terminal'
            ReleaseNotes = '0.7.10 - the status bar never worked on a machine that had not been used to develop it, and nothing anywhere said so. Zellij gates plugins behind a permission grant. Ungranted, zjstatus loads and is held pending approval, and the approval prompt renders IN THE PLUGIN OWN PANE - which the layout declares as one borderless row, in a session that starts locked. A dialog cannot draw in one row and no keystroke reaches that pane to accept it, so the outcome is no bar, no prompt, no error, and nothing in any log. The grant is acquired interactively, once, and cached in %LOCALAPPDATA%\Zellij\cache\permissions.kdl - outside the clone, outside %APPDATA%, and named nowhere in this repository until now. So every development machine had one and nothing that ships did, and no test could see the difference: it is state the published set could not carry, in a directory nothing looked at. Found on a second PC where the deployed layout was correct, the wasm was byte-identical to a working machine, the config set default_layout, the hook was demonstrably firing, and zt check reported No failures on every single line. install.ps1 now writes the grant beside the layout that references the plugin, because the permission that makes it load belongs in the same step as the file that names it. It MERGES rather than replacing: that file is Zellij own and can hold grants a person approved by hand, and silently revoking one reproduces this same invisible failure somewhere else. It is idempotent, keyed by the plugin absolute path with forward slashes - Zellij own form, taken from a file it wrote itself, so the grant is per machine and copying it between them grants nothing - and it is written even when the wasm is not there yet, since the grant is a statement about a path and downloading the plugin afterwards then works without a second install. That is the order the machine which hit this actually did it in. It also warns when a zellij server is running, because the server owns that file and writes its own copy back when it exits, so a grant written underneath a live session can be replaced by the state that session started with. zt check gains a zjstatus permitted row, which is the line that would have caught this on day one, and zt diag reads the cache directory it previously stopped one directory short of - the bundle listed %APPDATA%\Zellij and the answer was under %LOCALAPPDATA%. Nine assertions, six of them behavioural: they run the installer into a redirected profile and then run the layer check against the same redirected profile, so what is pinned is that the reader looks where the writer wrote rather than that three files contain matching text. One of them deletes the grant and asserts the new row reports FAIL, because a check that cannot fire is worse than no check and this entire entry exists because a table of passes described a machine with no status bar. 0.7.9 - zt diag, for the failure zt check cannot see. The layer check reports PASS on a machine where the rig does not work, and it is right to: every check in it asks whether a file is THERE, and the two files that decide whether a session starts are generated per machine and exist in no repository. %APPDATA%\Zellij\config\layouts\claude.kdl carries an absolute plugin path and an absolute cwd, both substituted at install time from a template. A layout that exists, parses, and points its plugin at a zjstatus.wasm nobody downloaded passes every question the layer check knows how to ask, and gives you a session with no status bar. An unreplaced {{PLUGINS}} does the same and takes the tab working directory with it. Neither prints anything, and install.ps1 says so in passing - it notes that the wasm is missing and carries on, so a clean install has no status bar by design. So zt diag collects rather than judges. It reads those files out verbatim and, where there is a source to compare against, regenerates what the installer should have written and diffs it - which on a machine with two clones names the clone that installed, in one line. Twelve sections: every ZellijTerminal on the module path and what each junction really resolves to, because a fix verified against the wrong tree looks exactly like a fix that works; zellij setup --check rather than our own Join-Path, since this project has already shipped one path missing the config level and a check that passed while reading nothing; the deployed layout and config; the plugin with a sha256, because the binary carries no version string worth trusting and a wasm built for a different plugin API loads, does nothing, and reports nothing; the hook registration with every stamped-in path tested; the registry; Windows Terminal; the environment; and finally zt check itself, so a clean table and a signal can be read side by side. The bundle opens with SIGNALS - observations, never verdicts, deduplicated - and is redacted by default, because it is written to be sent to somebody. It changes nothing on the machine. -ParseLayout is the single exception and says what it does: it is the only way to tell a KDL error from a missing session, because Zellij reports the first as the second, and --layout cannot be combined with --session, so the throwaway session it creates cannot be named in advance. The session list is captured before and after and only what appeared gets deleted. Two things the first run taught, both kept as comments where they happened. The layout template ships forty lines of commented worked examples, so scanning the raw text reported C:/code/api, api and web as tab directories that do not exist - four signals, every one of them prose. Every pattern now reads a comment-stripped copy, with the quote state tracked so a file:// location survives the stripping. And the escape-sequence cleaner uses [char]27 rather than the PowerShell 6 backtick-e escape: 5.1 does not error on an unknown backtick escape, it yields the literal character, so the pattern would have matched a letter e and quietly deleted real text out of the evidence. It parses either way, which is exactly why the 5.1 compat gate could not have caught it. 0.7.8 - zt setup could not complete a single one of its offers. Accept either of the two that call install.ps1 - write the Zellij config and layout, or register the hook for every project - and the installer got as far as its module step, printed the installed banner, and then died on "The term Write-Step is not recognized", a function it defines at the top of itself and had used four lines earlier. Nothing was wrong with install.ps1. A script invoked with the call operator from a MODULE function runs inside that module session state, and install.ps1 removes and re-imports ZellijTerminal as its first real step - which throws away the scope its own helpers were defined into, so every function it had not called yet stopped existing halfway through the run. Running install.ps1 from a shell was never affected, which is why the suite and every hand-run install stayed green: the guided path was the only caller with a module underneath it. It now starts a child pwsh. That is also the honest lifetime, because the installer replaces the module the guide is executing from and the copy already loaded stays loaded regardless - which is why install.ps1 has always ended by telling you to open a new shell. The child exit code is read, so an install that failed - a junction already pointing at a different clone, say - stops the guide walking on to the next step as though it had worked. The regression test rebuilds the mechanism from a module and a script of its own with no zt involved, because a fix is only worth keeping if the reason for it can still be demonstrated by somebody who was not there, and it pins the call shape both ways: the guard matches the old line and not the new one. On a clone that predates this, install.ps1 -Global run by hand from the clone does the same work and was never broken. 0.7.7 - Ctrl+V inside a Zellij pane pasted one line at a time, and in Claude Code every newline submits, so a ten-line paste became ten prompts. Windows Terminal wraps a paste in the bracketed-paste sequence only when the application it is talking to has enabled mode 2004, and inside a session that application is Zellij rather than the pane. Zellij 0.44.3 on Windows never asks, so Terminal types the clipboard in as ordinary keystrokes; Zellij never sees a paste either, so it cannot bracket the inner hop to the pane. One missing negotiation, both hops broken. It reads as a Claude Code bug and then as a TERM or NO_COLOR bug and is neither, and the way to prove the layer is to paste two lines at a plain pwsh prompt inside a pane with nothing else running. zt paste reports both layers and zt paste fix repairs them: Terminal stops owning ctrl+v so the keystroke reaches the application, and Claude Code gains a ctrl+v of its own pointed at chat:imagePaste, which despite the name is the clipboard read and handles text. Either half alone is worse than the bug, because Terminal unbound with no Claude binding leaves nothing pasting there at all. Deliberately not part of install.ps1: the installer reaches Terminal through fragments precisely so it never rewrites settings.json, which is JSONC and loses every comment to a ConvertTo-Json round trip. So the rewrite is a targeted text edit, it happens only when asked for by name, and it takes a backup first. alt+v always worked and still does. zt check gained two rows. Paste (Ctrl+V) reports the above, and a missing ctrl+v entry counts as broken: Terminal binds it by default, so reading silence as neutrality would report a clean bill of health on every unfixed machine. Release worktree FAILS when a registered workspace or a live session record sits inside a generated git worktree - Publish-Release.ps1 empties that directory on every run and rebuilds it from the manifest, so a session living there loses its files to the next publish and holds the directory open so the publish fails. Both have happened. It reads git worktree list rather than hard-coding a path, because that path is not a constant. Finally, a dead Claude session no longer reports as running. SessionEnd deletes the live record, the waiting flag, the status entry and the tab cache, but Claude Code cancels hooks that have not finished when it exits - Hook cancelled, for every hook registered, not just this one - and powershell.exe alone takes ~500 ms to start, so losing that race is normal rather than exceptional. The tab outlives the session too, because the pane drops back to a shell, and both survivors present read as running indefinitely, with zt go jumping to a dead session. The hook now records CLAUDE_PID, which Claude Code sets in the environment of everything it spawns and which costs nothing to read, so a reader can prove the session is gone; startedAt guards against a reused pid. The record is still KEPT when the process is dead, because leftover records are the entire input to zt restore, which reopens what a crash took down - deleting them would have turned shutdown recovery into a silent no-op. It reads as stale instead, and zt sync remains the only thing that removes it. The rule is written twice, in Core.ps1 and in the palette ZtStore.cs, because the palette reads these files directly and must not answer differently from zt. 0.7.6 - anyone who cloned before 0.7.5 could not update, and the failure named nothing that would explain itself. 0.7.5 rebuilt the published branch as a fresh orphan and force-pushed it, which is how a leaked blob is made unrecoverable and is expected to happen again. The cost lands on clones that already exist: their history is genuinely unrelated to the new one, so it can never fast-forward, and bootstrap.ps1 ran a bare git pull --ff-only. The one-liner therefore ended in "fatal: Not possible to fast-forward, aborting" and a failed install, permanently, for every returning user - reported from a machine still sitting on the pre-rebuild commit. It now fetches, tries the fast-forward, and on failure resets hard to the remote branch, saying that the history was rebuilt. It checks the tree is clean first and refuses while naming the files, because config/workspaces.json is tracked and is a file people reasonably edit in place. Deliberately no git clean: a clone from before 0.6 can still hold config/devices/<HOST>.json, a real device registry, untracked now only because that file moved to LOCALAPPDATA - and this project has lost that file to a clean once already. Four tests, one of which builds two real repositories and replaces the history of one, proving the fast-forward fails and the reset recovers rather than asserting it. If you are stuck on the old error, the fix cannot reach you through the thing that is broken: run git -C <clone> reset --hard origin/main once by hand, then the one-liner works again. 0.7.5 - the release an audit of the published repo paid for. The published history no longer carries a private lab note. A spike file shipped in 0.5.0 and deleted in 0.6.0 stayed recoverable from every clone, byte-identical to the private copy, while a shipped comment asserted that no spike is published and none ever will be. The branch was rebuilt as a fresh orphan and force-pushed - the tree is unchanged, so nothing anyone downloads differs - and the anonymisation gate now scans HISTORY as well as the tip: every path ever committed must still be on the manifest, and the personal-identifier needles run over every historical blob. It was run against the old history first, where it reports exactly the file that leaked. Related: zt hotkeys wrote its sample INTO a spikes directory inside the clone, creating the one directory this project promises is never published, with the device name in it; it goes to LOCALAPPDATA now. Seven places reported success that nothing had checked. Get-ZtTabNames returned the "Session not found" prose AS TAB NAMES, because query-tab-names prints it and exits 0. Test-ZtSession matched the session name as a substring of the whole session list, so with sessions claude and auspicious-pigeon live it answered yes to "auspicious", and would to "laud". zt stop announced "tab is now a shell" having observed nothing; it says what it SENT now. The pad path guard called ShortPath and never re-tested the result, which on a volume without 8.3 names returns the long path unchanged, so the remap was written and silently did nothing. The pad jump script wrote its state file after a go-to-tab-name that is a silent no-op when nothing is attached. bootstrap.ps1 never read the exit code install.ps1 sets for it, though install.ps1 names that omission in its own comment. And zj-claude-project.ps1 called zellij unguarded, so zt start without zellij threw the raw exception 0.7.4 claimed to have removed. Also: zt validate reported -Kind pwsh with no command as a fault, which is a documented, supported configuration. scripts/Test-Setup.ps1 promised Windows PowerShell 5.1 compatibility and used Start-Process -Environment, which arrived in 7.4 - so the one check written to prove the hook runs under 5.1 was guaranteed to fail there and only there. The hook corrected the tab name for a session running in a SUBFOLDER and then keyed its live record by the raw path anyway, so the workspace reported stopped while its session was plainly running and an orphan record was offered as a workspace of its own. The Command Palette extension passed -NonInteractive on every invocation including the visible ones, and under that flag Read-Host does not prompt - it fails and execution continues. So the five commands that end in "press enter to close" closed instantly, and "Define a root", which is two prompts, could never collect anything while still logging success. Its zellij helper redirected stderr and never read it, which can deadlock, with the timeout applied on the line after the one that blocks; kill and delete session reported success without checking; four user-visible actions wrote nothing to the log the README calls the first place to look; and zellij.exe was sought in exactly one directory before falling back to a PATH the module refuses to trust. pack.ps1 discarded the output of publish, makeappx and signtool, so a stranger whose build failed got a number and nothing else. The Windows SDK version, a hard build requirement, was documented only in a comment saying "only 10.0.26100.0 is installed here"; cmdpal/README.md now lists the prerequisites with their winget ids. Finally, the two surfaces share a vocabulary. The palette says "Unregister" and the CLI took only rm and remove - and remove routed to the unregister cmdlet while the cmdlet actually named Remove-ZellijTerminalTab was reached by close, so the one verb containing "remove" pointed at the opposite thing from the cmdlet named for it. Unregister-ZellijTerminal now answers to rm, remove, unregister and forget; Register-ZellijTerminal to add and register; tab completion knows them; and the documentation says in each place that the directory is never touched. 0.7.4 - zellij being absent is now a result rather than an exception. Every zt command that asked zellij anything went through one function that ran the executable without checking it was there, so on a machine where zellij is not installed - or simply not on PATH yet, which is the state install.ps1 exists to fix - the answer was a raw CommandNotFoundException from somewhere deep in the module instead of anything a person could act on. Get-ZtClientCount now reports -1, Test-ZtSession reports false, and zt sessions returns nothing, which is what all three of those already meant by "could not answer". Found by CI rather than by anyone using it: a hosted runner has no zellij, and the suite had been green on every machine that had the rig installed - which is exactly the population structurally unable to notice. The regression test empties PATH rather than filtering it, so it does not depend on where zellij happens to live. 0.7.3 - zac was blind to two thirds of Windows Terminal, and the check meant to catch that could never have fired. The window check matched process name WindowsTerminal only; Preview is WindowsTerminalPreview and Canary is WindowsTerminalCanary, so on either channel zac read the machine as having no window open and opened a SECOND client on a session that already had one - mirrored, with the grid pinned to the smaller, every time, with no self-correction. It then compared the Terminal window count before and after wt focus-tab to decide whether the focus was real. Terminal hosts every window in ONE PROCESS: opening a second window leaves that count at 1 and simply moves the MainWindowHandle of the process. The count could never rise, so the comparison reported success unconditionally and the branch handling the failure was unreachable - a check that cannot fail, which is worse than no check because it reads like evidence. Demonstrated rather than reasoned about, as was the reason the old comment was wrong: wt -w <unknown> focus-tab exits 0 and does NOTHING, watched for three seconds. focus-tab does not conjure windows; new-tab does. That check is gone. In its place is a question that can be answered: a zellij client is a PROCESS, so walking up from the tab running zellij attach reaches the terminal hosting it. zac now asks whether a Windows Terminal is showing THIS session, where before it asked whether any Terminal window existed anywhere on the machine - which was true while the client was attached from a different terminal application entirely. Nothing claims to have verified a focus any more, because there is no observable for one. A second bug, found by writing the test rather than by reading the code: zellij prints Session x not found and STILL EXITS 0, and the client parse counted every line that was not the header - so three lines of prose read as three attached clients and a session that does not exist reported as attached. Rows are counted by shape now, and the count returns -1 when zellij could not answer, so no such session and nobody watching can no longer be confused for each other. The cold path stops recording its window on the wt exit code, which is 0 the moment the tab is handed off and says nothing about whether zellij attach ever ran; the marker is written only once a client has actually arrived. The window name defaults to the session name, so attaching a second session from the palette no longer raises the window belonging to the first. tests/Attach.Tests.ps1 adds 27 assertions over the client parse, the Terminal firstWindowPreference read, and the decisions zac has to keep making. Documentation that could not be followed as written: zellij/README.md gave Zellij paths missing the config level and named a claude.kdl that is not in the repo - the layout ships as a template because it carries absolute paths - and docs/05-usage.md told you to copy that same missing file, so the by-hand install path was broken for anyone not running install.ps1. The user guide advertised zt hotkeys -Install, which is exported nowhere and reachable from no dispatch route; printed a sessions example with a literal list where a session name belongs; showed a workspace table three columns out of date that listed one id twice at one path, which the registry cannot hold; and began at <title> with no doctype and no charset while carrying 48 non-ASCII characters. Four published files justified the no-Ctrl+Alt rule by citing a development notes file that is never published, so the pointer was unfollowable by construction; they now cite D21 in docs/02-decisions.md, which states the same rule in public. The reference check that missed them was wrong three ways - the pattern could not see a bare filename, the question it asked was does this exist here rather than will the reader have it, and an extension allow-list hid the .ahk and .cs sites - and after fixing it was run against the previous commit to prove it reports all four. docs/00-background.md now records the mirroring behaviour itself rather than leaving it in a troubleshooting entry, including the consequence that appeared in no document at all: each client carries its OWN focus, so an injected keystroke can land in a pane you are not looking at. 0.7.2 - the tab says what its session is doing. A Claude tab is now named claude-web-api ~ while it reads, # while it writes, v when it has finished and wants you - the same table that colours the status bar, which could only ever say WHICH project was busy and never point at a tab. This was supposed to be impossible: rename-tab renames the FOCUSED tab, so a background hook calling it would rename whatever you were looking at. It turns out to take -t <id>, which targets a tab and leaves focus alone; docs/00-background.md had recorded the opposite as confirmed, and now records the retest. Colour cannot come along - zjstatus substitutes a tab name as plain text, so markup in it prints literally. The consequence to know about if you script against this rig: a tab s live name is no longer its identity. Strip the glyph before comparing and resolve back to the live name before go-to-tab-name, which matches exactly and no-ops silently on a miss. A session working in a SUBFOLDER of its project is now labelled by the project rather than by the folder: it used to invent a tab name that existed nowhere, which meant the one session actually asking for attention was the one key 3 refused to jump to. zac stops opening a second window on a session something is already viewing - Zellij mirrors, so that is one session with two clients, the same pane in both, your keystrokes in both, and the grid pinned to the SMALLEST client, which presents as a window that will not resize and text that will not reflow. The Windows Terminal tab is titled zellij-terminal and carries Zellij s icon, via a profile contributed as a Terminal FRAGMENT so your own settings.json is never edited; the icon is extracted from your installed zellij.exe rather than shipped here, and both are removed on uninstall. Terminal reads fragments at startup, so restart it once. zt check gained three lines: more than one client attached, Terminal firstWindowPreference (persistedWindowLayout restores a window already running zellij attach and then honours your command line on top of it, which is where the duplicate window came from), and it no longer counts any client as healthy. 0.7.1 - zac no longer opens a second window on a session something is already viewing. Zellij mirrors, so a second client is not a second session: it shows the same pane, takes your keystrokes in both, and sizes the grid to the SMALLEST client attached - which presents as a window that will not resize and text that will not reflow, and sends you looking at the terminal emulator rather than at the client list. It now tries the named window handle first and reads the Terminal window count to find out whether that focused a window or created one, because wt exits 0 either way. The Windows Terminal TAB is titled zellij-terminal rather than the session name; the session name was already on the zellij tab bar directly below it. zt check gained two lines: one when more than one client is attached, naming the resize symptom, and one for Terminal firstWindowPreference, because persistedWindowLayout restores a window that is already running zellij attach and then honours the command line on top of it - a cold attach opens two windows and nothing inside this rig can see why. 0.7.0 - the Command Palette extension had drifted eight verbs behind the module; it can now reach sync, publish, config (device and shared), validate, roots, define a root, export and import, plus previous tab. The rarer ones sit on a "registry and config" page rather than adding eight entries to a palette root shared with every other extension. Every palette action now logs its command and exit code to %LOCALAPPDATA%\ZellijTerminal\palette.log - the action path previously created no window and ended in an empty catch, so two verbs were completely dead for a day behind a toast that looked like success. Commands that block on Read-Host now get a console window automatically, decided in the constructor rather than per call site: "check the rig" had promised output "in a window" while running with CreateNoWindow, so it rendered where nobody could see and hung on its own prompt. 0.6.0 - the per-device registry moved OUT of the clone to %LOCALAPPDATA%\ZellijTerminal\devices\<HOST>.json. It is state this machine writes rather than source that ships, and in a working tree it died to git clean -xfd, to a re-clone, or to the directory being rebuilt underneath it. Nothing migrates automatically: copy your old config\devices\<HOST>.json to the new path, or set $env:ZT_CONFIG_HOME to the old directory to keep the previous layout - that variable relocates BOTH config files together and is how several PCs share one list through a private repo. config\workspaces.json is unchanged, because it is committed content that ships with the clone. zt check now prints which registry file it read, not just how many workspaces are in it. 0.5.0 - zt setup, a guided walkthrough that explains each layer before offering to do it and can install Zellij with winget; zt pad explain and zt palette, so the two optional pieces explain themselves rather than looking like broken required ones; the hook records its own failures to a log that zt check reports, closing the one place this rig could still fail silently; install.ps1 -Global registers the hook for every project rather than one repo, merged into existing settings; install.ps1 gates on PowerShell 7 and offers to install Zellij; zt add says which kind it registered and what will run, and -Kind pwsh no longer requires a command; Claude session display names drop the claude- tab prefix; a Pester suite and CI. 0.4.0 - zt pick and tab completion after zt; park/restore, so a shutdown is recovered from rather than caught; config/validate for hand-editing the JSON; an AGE column so a forgotten session is visible. 0.3.0 - renamed from ClaudeZellij to ZellijTerminal (zt). Adds a workspace registry split across a shared config in git and a per-device config only that device writes, live state under LOCALAPPDATA, start/stop/restart/close, tab-name collision detection, argument completion, and a zt dispatcher. 0.2.0 - Connect (zac). 0.1.0 - first cut wrapping the scripts.'
        }
    }
}










